# Knowledge Compactor

Spec: KnowledgeCompactor
Purpose:
  Define the knowledge compactor — a process that combines fragmented
  knowledge into canonical concepts with evidence links. Distinct from
  the garbage collector (which removes waste), the compactor combines
  knowledge that is related but scattered.

Owns: lifecycle/compactor.sdd

## Compactor vs Garbage Collector

  Garbage Collector (gc-policy.sdd):
    removes waste: broken refs, temp indexes, obsolete metadata
    scope: cleanup, deletion, archival
    direction: reduce (fewer items)

  Compactor:
    combines fragmentation: merges related knowledge into canonical forms
    scope: synthesis, consolidation, canonicalization
    direction: compress (same knowledge, fewer representations)

  [COMPACT-01] The compactor NEVER deletes — it merges and redirects.
  [COMPACT-02] The GC NEVER merges — it only proposes archival/deletion.
  [COMPACT-03] Both operate at AUTONOMY L1 (Suggest) in the autonomy policy.

## Compaction Protocol

  DISCOVERIES
    ↓
  CLUSTER
    ↓
  COMMON PATTERN
    ↓
  CONCEPT
    ↓
  EVIDENCE LINKS

  1. DISCOVERIES: AI finds N independent observations of the same pattern
     Example: 50 projects show "DB transaction + event publishing" inconsistency

  2. CLUSTER: Group observations by semantic similarity
     - Same domain + same problem = same cluster
     - Different domain + different problem = different cluster

  3. COMMON PATTERN: Extract the shared invariant across all observations
     Example: "Transaction and event must be atomic or retried together"

  4. CONCEPT: Create canonical @concept.* with the common pattern
     Example: @concept.transaction-event-consistency

  5. EVIDENCE LINKS: Attach all N observations as evidence to the concept
     Example:
       evidence:
         - @observation.O55
         - @observation.O56
         ...
         - @observation.O104

  Before:
    50 independent observations scattered across tmp/ and insights/
  After:
    1 @concept.* + 50 @observation.* evidence links

## Core + Variant Specialization

  Pattern:
    @skill.retry                    (core — general retry logic)
          ↑
    @skill.payment-retry            (specializes @skill.retry)
    @skill.provider-retry           (specializes @skill.retry)

  specialization fields:
    core:
      @skill.retry                  (the abstract parent)
    specialization:
      @skill.payment-retry          (the concrete child)
    relation: specializes           (explicit typed edge, NOT inheritance)
    variant_of:
      @claim.C11                    (the claim this variant serves)

  [CORE-01] Core skills define the general pattern; variants add context.
  [CORE-02] Specialization is via explicit `specializes:` edge, not deep extends chains.
  [CORE-03] Deep extends chains (A extends B extends C extends D) are prohibited —
            context resolution becomes intractable beyond 2 levels.
  [CORE-04] Each variant MUST reference its core via `specializes:` or `extends:`.

## Merge Proposal

  When the compactor detects two or more entities that are near-duplicates
  (semantic similarity > 90%), it generates a merge proposal:

  @merge.M22

  source:
    - @skill.payment-retry
    - @skill.retry-payment

  proposal:
    merge

  reason:
    Same scope and same behavior; payment-retry already has more evidence

  confidence:
    0.96

  requires:
    review

  Rules:
    [MERGE-01] AI CANNOT auto-merge — must propose and wait for approval.
    [MERGE-02] Merge proposals with confidence < 0.90 MUST require human review.
    [MERGE-03] On merge, the surviving entity absorbs aliases of the deprecated one.
    [MERGE-04] Deprecated entity is marked DEPRECATED with a supersedes pointer.
    [MERGE-05] Original entities are NEVER deleted — archived with supersedes link.

## Evidence Compression

  Evidence entities may themselves be duplicate or compressible:

  Before:
    @evidence.E1 — source: R44, claims: [C1]
    @evidence.E2 — source: R44, claims: [C2]
    @evidence.E3 — source: R44, claims: [C3]

  After (compressed):
    @evidence.source.R44
      claims:
        - @claim.C1
        - @claim.C2
        - @claim.C3

  [EVID-01] Evidence from the same source MAY be compressed into a single
            evidence entity with multiple claims.
  [EVID-02] Compressed evidence retains individual claim pointers.

## Alias Mechanism

  Canonical entity with aliases:

  @term.application-service
    canonical_name: Application Service
    aliases:
      - service
      - app-service
      - use-case-service
      - application layer service

  When AI encounters "service layer" in code, it resolves to
  @term.application-service via alias matching.

  [ALIAS-01] Aliases MUST map to exactly one canonical entity.
  [ALIAS-02] Reference resolution MUST try exact match, then alias, then
             semantic search, then ask human.
  [ALIAS-03] Terminology normalization creates @observation.terminology-drift
             when codebase terms drift from policy terms.

## Specialization (Not Inheritance)

  Deep specialization chains are avoided:

  BAD:
    @skill.retry
      ↑ extends
    @skill.payment-retry
      ↑ extends
    @skill.stripe-payment-retry
      ↑ extends
    @skill.stripe-payment-retry-v2

  GOOD:
    @skill.retry                        (core)
      ↑ specializes
    @skill.payment-retry                (specialization)
      ↑ specializes
    @skill.stripe-payment-retry         (specialization)

  Each level is a flat specialization with at most 1 core parent.

